{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"better_next_step_if_any\":\"The authority checks expose two additional synthesis gaps: the proposed receipt still has no authenticated human-approval mechanism, and “protected integration commit” is undefined in this repository—the frozen HEAD is on `provider-adapters`, while local `main` is behind. I’m reading the canonical pipeline decision documents now to distinguish an intentional policy boundary from an accidental omission before finalizing the critique.\",\"failure_if_followed\":[\"A typed JSON manifest can remain a self-authored assertion if human approval is just a name/prose field.\",\"Post-merge validation cannot prove integration unless the integration ref and its protection/ownership are explicit.\"],\"final_assessment\":\"synthesis_needs_revision\",\"hidden_assumptions\":[\"That a compact receipt becomes authoritative merely by being typed.\",\"That a protected integration branch and authenticated approval channel already exist.\"],\"ignored_minority_arguments\":[\"The workflow review warned that `approved_by` is only an assertion and that lifecycle transitions need idempotent, compare-and-set semantics; the synthesis does not resolve either point.\"],\"reason\":\"The delivery-first direction remains compelling, but its proposed authority mechanism is incomplete.\",\"strongest_counter_case\":\"Use an ordinary human-owned branch/merge workflow and treat the existing Unity XML plus merge review as evidence before inventing a new ledger authority. Define the integration ref and approval provenance first.\",\"synthesis_weaknesses\":[\"The receipt/journal storage, atomicity, and authority are unspecified.\",\"The exact-commit validation story has no named integration ref or branch-protection evidence.\"],\"what_would_change_my_mind\":[]}"}}
