{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba 'Docs/AI-Pipeline/START_HERE.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# START HERE — No Safe Circle AI Pipeline\n     2\t\n     3\tThis is the first file any AI assistant or developer should read before working on the autonomous development pipeline.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Purpose\n     6\t\n     7\tThe pipeline is built across multiple work sessions and AI contexts. Do not rely on conversation memory as the source of truth.\n     8\t\n     9\t**The repository is the source of truth.**\n    10\t\n    11\t## Current Status Snapshot\n    12\t\n    13\tStage 4B.2 practical repository read/search and Stage 4B.3 AgentRuntime/TaskExecution separation remain complete on `provider-adapters`. The current implementation slice is **Stage 4C — OpenAI/Codex AgentRuntime and ArchitectureReview migration**. The initial fail-closed `ClaudeCodeProvider` and shared bounded `StandardProcessRunner` were committed at:\n    14\t\n    15\t```text\n    16\tae046fd828f168dac6c87c49878fe1812f6c1fd7\n    17\t```\n    18\t\n    19\tThe provider preserves structured-output invocations with empty capabilities and now also supports:\n    20\t\n    21\t```text\n    22\trepository_read -> Claude Read\n    23\trepository_search -> Claude Glob and Grep\n    24\tboth -> Claude Read, Glob, and Grep\n    25\ttoken_limit = null\n    26\t```\n    27\t\n    28\tEmpty-capability invocations still run in a fresh empty temporary workspace with no tools. Repository-capable Claude invocations run in the actual repository root (`/workspace` in Docker) and may accept validated repository-relative `context_paths` as prompt guidance. Repository writing, AgentRuntime `approved_command_execution`, and web access remain unsupported. Codex ArchitectureReview read/search uses ordinary read-only shell/file inspection inside the externally read-only `codex-review` Docker environment; that is not approved command execution. See ADR-038 for the trusted single-user boundary and accepted limited read-containment risk.\n    29\t\n    30\tThe deterministic AgentRuntime, process-runner, and Claude-provider suites all pass. Before Stage 4C work, the opt-in live Claude repository read/search smoke also passed through `AgentRunner`. This proves the Claude adapter path; it does not mean the direct Claude ArchitectureReview runner has migrated to AgentRuntime.\n    31\t\n    32\tStage 4C implements OpenAI/Codex behind generic AgentRuntime and migrates ArchitectureReview as the first real existing non-task consumer. Codex supports empty capability, repository read, repository search, and their combination; repository access requires an explicit externally enforced read-only profile and ArchitectureReview relies on the `codex-review` Docker mount. The approved timeout translation is:\n    33\t\n    34\t```text\n    35\teffective_timeout_seconds = min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\tThis is a bounded-execution policy, not a cross-provider-equivalent turn measurement.\n    39\t\n    40\tAgentRuntime exposes generic `AgentInvocationRequest` with no dependency on NSC task IDs or `Tasks/*.yaml`; TaskExecution separately owns NSC task identity. ArchitectureReview now reaches Codex through `AgentRunner` without TaskExecution. ExecutionCrew, Reconciliation migration, provider fallback, and write/command authority remain out of scope.\n    41\t\n    42\tArchitectureReview generated output is provider-scoped under `outputs/claude/` and `outputs/codex/`; each provider-specific latest view includes convenience copies of its synthesis and critique. Global `outputs/latest/` contains only one atomic `LATEST.json` pointer to the most recently completed provider-scoped run. Independent reviewers ignore prior review opinions even when architecture/current-state documents summarize them, while still evaluating implemented architecture, accepted decisions, and current facts. Both provider paths retain eight independent reviewer roles. The separation preserves provenance and enables later Claude-versus-Codex comparison; no comparison pipeline exists yet.\n    43\t\n    44\tAs of 2026-08-21:\n    45\t\n    46\t- **Milestone 1 — Persistent Work Graph is COMPLETE.**\n    47\t- The approved persistent graph contains 37 work records under `Tasks/`.\n    48\t- `taskcontrol validate`, `list`, `show`, `ready`, and `graph` work against the real persisted graph.\n    49\t- The first deterministic ready frontier contains seven executable `single_agent` implementation tasks.\n    50\t- Reconciliation/verification history is immutable; `Tasks/*.yaml` is the living operational work graph.\n    51\t- The one-time bootstrap is complete and must not be rerun.\n    52\t- Current `main` has been merged into `adversarial-architecture-review` so the architecture-review branch includes the completed Milestone 1 implementation.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe next planned infrastructure milestone is **Milestone 2 — RAG + Project Scanner + Compact Context + Progressive Decomposition + Artifact Authority**.\n    55\t\n    56\tBefore a large Milestone 2 investment, use the adversarial architecture review as a checkpoint against the now-M1-complete repository:\n    57\t\n    58\t`Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/README.md`\n    59\t\n    60\tThe next infrastructure work should also advance a real No Safe Circle task rather than creating another infrastructure-only stretch.\n    61\t\n    62\t## Required Reading Order\n    63\t\n    64\t1. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md`.\n    65\t2. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/08_STAGE_4B_CLAUDE_CODE_PROVIDER.md` for the latest provider implementation state.\n    66\t3. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-037_CLAUDE_CODE_PROVIDER_BOUNDARY.md` and `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-038_PRACTICAL_REPOSITORY_READ_SEARCH.md` for the Claude boundaries.\n    67\t4. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md` for the target architecture.\n    68\t5. Read the milestone/context file named by `CURRENT_STATE.md`.\n    69\t6. Read `Docs/AI-Pipeline/DECISIONS.md` when the work touches architecture, Git workflow, task semantics, autonomy, RAG, GER, evaluation, refinement, validation, progressive decomposition, or artifact authority.\n    70\t7. Inspect the actual repository state before changing anything.\n    71\t\n    72\tAny work touching Unity tests, validation harnesses, scenes, prefabs, builders/generators, or evidence-producing Unity runs must also read `Docs/Engineering/UNITY_TESTING_POLICY.md`.\n    73\t\n    74\tFor current post-M1 work, the primary milestone context is:\n    75\t\n    76\t`Docs/AI-Pipeline/02_RAG_SCANNER_CONTEXT.md`\n    77\t\n    78\t`01_MILESTONE_TASK_GRAPH.md` is now a **completed milestone record and semantic reference**, not the active implementation plan.\n    79\t\n    80\tDo not read every milestone file unless you need broader architecture context.\n    81\t\n    82\t## Milestone Routing Table\n    83\t\n    84\tCurrent work | Read this file\n    85\t--- | ---\n    86\tCompleted persistent graph semantics, stable IDs, readiness, taskcontrol | `01_MILESTONE_TASK_GRAPH.md`\n    87\t**Current next milestone:** RAG canon retrieval, Unity/code scanner, context packs, progressive decomposition, artifact authority | `02_RAG_SCANNER_CONTEXT.md`\n    88\tSupervisor, task claiming, Git branches, worktrees, GitHub Issues/Projects/PRs | `03_SUPERVISOR_GIT_GITHUB_CONTEXT.md`\n    89\tAssignment 3 crew, Assignment 6 GER, bounded repair loops, deterministic tests, Unity/runtime validation, evaluator specializations | `04_EXECUTION_GER_VALIDATION_CONTEXT.md`\n    90\tProvider-neutral AgentRuntime, Claude/OpenAI adapters, and production Execution Crew architecture | `06_PROVIDER_NEUTRAL_EXECUTION_CREW_PLAN.md`\n    91\tProvider-adapter implementation, capability mapping, and fail-closed enforcement | `07_PROVIDER_ADAPTER_CAPABILITY_MAPPING.md` and `ADR-035_PROVIDER_ADAPTER_ENFORCEMENT.md`\n    92\tCompleted Stage 4B Claude Code provider and accepted containment/lifecycle boundary | `08_STAGE_4B_CLAUDE_CODE_PROVIDER.md` and `ADR-037_CLAUDE_CODE_PROVIDER_BOUNDARY.md`\n    93\tContinuous autonomous ticket processing, budgets, blockers, parallel workers, planning refresh | `05_CONTINUOUS_AUTONOMY_CONTEXT.md`\n    94\t\n    95\t## Milestone 1 Commands\n    96\t\n    97\tThe completed persistent graph can be inspected locally with:\n    98\t\n    99\t```text\n   100\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py validate\n   101\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py list\n   102\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py show NSC-003\n   103\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py ready\n   104\tpython Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py graph\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\tDo **not** rerun the initial persistent-graph bootstrap. `Pipeline/TaskGraph/BOOTSTRAP_PERSISTED.json` marks it complete.\n   108\t\n   109\t## Current First Real Task Anchor\n   110\t\n   111\tThe current graph already has executable work. A strong first gameplay/context-pipeline anchor is:\n   112\t\n   113\t`NSC-003 — Mouse-Directed Player Movement, Shared Pointer Projection, and Movement Restriction`\n   114\t\n   115\tIt is `open`, `implementation`, `single_agent`, and has no dependencies.\n   116\t\n   117\tUse real near-term work such as this to test compact context generation and later execution orchestration. Do not build a generic context/supervisor platform while leaving the game untouched.\n   118\t\n   119\t## Important GER Lesson\n   120\t\n   121\tAssignment 6 demonstrated that GER is not only a gate for generated content.\n   122\t\n   123\tFor No Safe Circle, the successful loop was:\n   124\t\n   125\t`bounded task → implement → evaluate → collect validation/runtime feedback → refine → re-evaluate → approve or circuit-break`\n   126\t\n   127\tThe camera implementation passed static evaluation before it was actually usable in Unity. Runtime failures were converted into structured feedback and sent back through the Refiner.\n   128\t\n   129\tFuture execution work must therefore treat runtime evidence as first-class validation input rather than assuming source-level success means the feature works.\n   130\t\n   131\tReference:\n   132\t\n   133\t- `Assignment6GER/README_Assignment6.md`\n   134\t- `Docs/AI-Pipeline/00_MASTER_CONTEXT.md`\n   135\t- `Docs/AI-Pipeline/DECISIONS.md`\n   136\t\n   137\t## Important Progressive-Decomposition Lesson\n   138\t\n   139\tThe task graph does not need the entire game decomposed into low-level implementation tickets in advance.\n   140\t\n   141\tWhen high-level work approaches the actionable frontier, the Progressive Decomposer determines why it is not yet a safe one-agent handoff.\n   142\t\n   143\tThere are two distinct cases:\n   144\t\n   145\t1. **Execution-size problem** — approved design is concrete, but the implementation record is too broad. Split only the already-approved responsibilities.\n   146\t2. **Design-information problem** — approved design/content is missing. Propose the smallest missing artifact instead of inventing the design.\n   147\t\n   148\tDetecting missing design and generating missing design are separate actions.\n   149\t\n   150\tArtifact creation must pass the Artifact Authority Gate before generation.\n   151\t\n   152\t## Important Artifact-Authority Lesson\n   153\t\n   154\tAn AI-generated artifact is not trusted merely because it exists.\n   155\t\n   156\tThe trust path is:\n   157\t\n   158\t```text\n   159\tMissing approved design detected\n   160\t        ↓\n   161\tArtifact proposal\n   162\t        ↓\n   163\tArtifact Authority Gate\n   164\t        ↓\n   165\tAuthorized generation\n   166\t        ↓\n   167\tArtifact GER / required evaluators\n   168\t        ↓\n   169\tApproved artifact\n   170\t        ↓\n   171\tTrusted downstream context\n   172\t```\n   173\t\n   174\tApproved artifacts may add authorized detail where the GDD leaves room, but they may not contradict or silently replace root canon.\n   175\t\n   176\t## Important Reconciliation-Snapshot Lesson\n   177\t\n   178\tReconciliation is not the mutable task database.\n   179\t\n   180\tEach full Reconciliation Agent run produces an immutable point-in-time snapshot of GDD requirements versus repository state.\n   181\t\n   182\tThe initial persistent graph was seeded only after independent verification and human approval.\n   183\t\n   184\tLater implementation work updates the persistent graph; it does not rewrite old reconciliation snapshots.\n   185\t\n   186\tA later reconciliation creates a new snapshot and may propose a graph delta. That delta must cross an explicit review/diff/apply boundary before changing persistent work.\n   187\t\n   188\tSafe cascading readiness changes remain deterministic `taskcontrol` behavior, not direct LLM graph rewrites.\n   189\t\n   190\t## Independent Reconciliation Verification Gate\n   191\t\n   192\tA successful Reconciliation Agent run is a candidate snapshot, not automatic graph truth.\n   193\t\n   194\tBefore the initial bootstrap, the multi-model Reconciliation Verification Crew independently audited:\n   195\t\n   196\t- GDD coverage;\n   197\t- dependency/decomposition semantics;\n   198\t- repository evidence;\n   199\t- execution scope / one-agent handoff size.\n   200\t\n   201\tMaterial findings were unioned rather than majority-voted, repaired through bounded refinement, and independently re-verified before human approval.\n   202\t\n   203\tThe approved bootstrap source ended with zero material findings.\n   204\t\n   205\tThis verification architecture remains available for later substantial reconciliation events; do not rerun it for every ordinary implementation ticket.\n   206\t\n   207\t## Current-Output Convention\n   208\t\n   209\tUse:\n   210\t\n   211\t`Pipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/current/`\n   212\t\n   213\tfor the latest human-facing reconciliation/verification status.\n   214\t\n   215\tTreat:\n   216\t\n   217\t`Pipeline/Reconciliation/outputs/runs/`\n   218\t\n   219\tas immutable audit history.\n   220\t\n   221\tDo not use mutable `outputs/current/` as authority for the already-approved initial bootstrap. The approval manifest binds the specific immutable verification artifacts used for that seed.\n   222\t\n   223\t## Core Principle\n   224\t\n   225\t**Use deterministic local tools for facts and computation. Use LLMs for judgment and bounded implementation.**\n   226\t\n   227\tThe local supervisor will eventually own the autonomous loop. Claude or another coding model is a bounded worker operating on one selected piece of work at a time.\n   228\t\n   229\tA worker does not declare itself successful. Project evidence does.\n   230\t\n   231\tAn implementation worker also does not silently create new game design. Missing design becomes an explicit artifact proposal.\n   232\t\n   233\t## Current Development Rule\n   234\t\n   235\tFrom this point forward, pipeline implementation should be tested by advancing the actual game.\n   236\t\n   237\tPrefer this pattern:\n   238\t\n   239\t```text\n   240\treal ready/near-ready No Safe Circle work\n   241\t        ↓\n   242\timplement only the infrastructure needed for that work\n   243\t        ↓\n   244\tuse it on the real task\n   245\t        ↓\n   246\tmeasure what failed / what was missing\n   247\t        ↓\n   248\tadd the next bounded infrastructure slice\n   249\t```\n   250\t\n   251\tAvoid building later autonomous layers speculatively before the preceding single-task path has proven reliable.\n   252\t\n   253\t## End-of-Session Rule\n   254\t\n   255\tBefore ending a meaningful pipeline work session:\n   256\t\n   257\t1. Update `CURRENT_STATE.md` when the milestone/slice changed.\n   258\t2. Update the active milestone completion/status notes when appropriate.\n   259\t3. Add an entry to `DECISIONS.md` if an architectural decision changed.\n   260\t4. Ensure commands and newly-created files are documented.\n   261\t5. Commit the documentation with the implementation it describes.\n   262\t\n   263\tA new AI window should be able to resume by reading the repository without needing the previous chat transcript.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
