{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba Docs/AI-Pipeline/CURRENT_STATE.md | sed -n '1,380p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# CURRENT STATE — No Safe Circle AI Pipeline\n     2\t\n     3\t> Update this file whenever a milestone or important implementation slice changes.\n     4\t\n     5\tLast updated: 2026-08-23, for Stage 4C OpenAI/Codex AgentRuntime and ArchitectureReview migration on `provider-adapters`.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Current Phase\n     8\t\n     9\t**Architecture Correction Phase 3 — COMPLETE AND MERGED.**\n    10\t\n    11\tPhase 3A — Evidence-Derived Current Conformance Evaluator — is complete.\n    12\t\n    13\tPhase 3B — First Real Production Baseline — is complete.\n    14\t\n    15\tPhase 3 was fast-forward merged into `main` at:\n    16\t\n    17\t```text\n    18\t43fdf0a163e281204906abd43a241db211587a0f\n    19\t```\n    20\t\n    21\t**Provider-Neutral Execution Crew Plan — Stage 4A: MAPPING, ENFORCEMENT, AND TRANSPORT-EVALUATION DECISIONS COMPLETE.**\n    22\t\n    23\tThe current development branch is:\n    24\t\n    25\t```text\n    26\tprovider-adapters\n    27\t```\n    28\t\n    29\tThe Stage 3 foundation now implements:\n    30\t\n    31\t- provider-neutral immutable `AgentInvocationRequest` and `AgentResult` contracts;\n    32\t- semantic capability and write-boundary contracts;\n    33\t- `low_cost`, `standard`, and `high_reasoning` model capability classes;\n    34\t- strict provider configuration loading and validation;\n    35\t- bounded request budgets and normalized failure classifications;\n    36\t- strict JSON value and supported-schema validation;\n    37\t- atomic, no-overwrite immutable run artifacts;\n    38\t- provider-neutral base interfaces;\n    39\t- a deterministic, side-effect-free `FakeProvider`;\n    40\t- an adversarial regression suite covering provider trust boundaries, path safety, schema failures, immutable artifacts, malformed provider values, and historical-directory protection.\n    41\t\n    42\tThe Stage 3 implementation is integrated; Stage 4A changes are documentation and decision work only.\n    43\t\n    44\tLive CLI discovery is complete. Non-production-write structured-output success probes completed for Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex. The provider-transport adopt-versus-build spike is also complete: `agent-mux` and `agent-shell` were evaluated and are not being adopted as production dependencies. ADR-035 was amended on 2026-08-23 to keep `AgentRequest` schema 1.0 and its required integer `turn_limit`, with explicit provider-specific enforcement mappings.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Stage 4B.2 — Practical Repository Read/Search** extends the existing Claude adapter for this trusted single-user local environment. `repository_read` maps to Claude `Read`; `repository_search` maps to Claude `Glob` and `Grep`; their combination exposes all three. Repository-capable calls use the actual source repository root (`/workspace` in Docker), while empty-capability calls retain their fresh empty temporary workspace and no-tool behavior. Validated `context_paths` are accepted only with repository capability and become prompt guidance rather than a hardened sandbox boundary.\n    47\t\n    48\t**Stage 4C — OpenAI/Codex Provider and ArchitectureReview Migration** adds `OpenAICodexProvider` behind generic AgentRuntime. The active OpenAI ArchitectureReview path now constructs task-neutral `AgentInvocationRequest` values and calls `AgentRunner`; provider subprocess, JSONL/final-output parsing, usage normalization, timeout translation, and failure normalization live in AgentRuntime. Invocation audit artifacts are nested under each ArchitectureReview run. TaskExecution is not involved.\n    49\t\n    50\tAgentRuntime `approved_command_execution`, repository writing, web access, non-null token limits, provider fallback, and production orchestration remain unsupported. The Codex ArchitectureReview repository read/search implementation uses Codex's ordinary read-only shell/file inspection inside the externally read-only `codex-review` Docker environment; that is not approved command execution. Stage 4C remains the OpenAI/Codex Provider stage.\n    51\t\n    52\tArchitectureReview generated runs and provider-latest views are separated under `outputs/claude/` and `outputs/codex/`; those provider-specific latest views retain convenience copies. Global `outputs/latest/` contains only one atomic `LATEST.json` pointer identifying the most recently completed provider-scoped run across providers. Manifests record the explicit provider namespace, resume cannot cross it, and failed/partial runs do not publish latest. Each provider retains eight independent reviewer roles. New independent reviewers ignore prior review opinions even when summarized in architecture/current-state documentation, while still using implemented architecture, accepted decisions, and current documented facts as evidence to judge independently. This preserves review provenance and supports a later Claude-versus-Codex comparison without implementing that comparison pipeline.\n    53\t\n    54\t**Provider-Neutral Execution Crew Plan — Stage 4: Implement Claude Code and OpenAI/Codex adapters against the same AgentRuntime fixtures.**\n    55\t\n    56\tThe accepted Stage 4 turn-budget mapping is:\n    57\t\n    58\t- Claude: `turn_limit=N` -> native `--max-turns N`, also bounded by external `timeout_seconds`;\n    59\t- OpenAI/Codex: no observed native turn limit, so use temporary `OPENAI_SECONDS_PER_TURN = 30` and `effective_timeout_seconds = min(timeout_seconds, turn_limit * 30)`;\n    60\t- these mappings are bounded-execution controls, not cross-provider-equivalent units of model work;\n    61\t- any non-null `token_limit` remains unsupported and fails closed.\n    62\t\n    63\tNo production `ExecutionCrew` role orchestration, Unity execution by an agent, GER integration, task selection, dependency readiness, autonomous dispatch, automatic Git commit/merge behavior, or live provider fallback has been enabled.\n    64\t\n    65\tArchitecture Correction Phase 3 remains intentionally fail-closed for execution:\n    66\t\n    67\t```text\n    68\tTASK READINESS: UNAVAILABLE — DISPATCH POLICY NOT ENABLED\n    69\t```\n    70\t\n    71\tand:\n    72\t\n    73\t```text\n    74\tEXECUTION AUTHORIZATION: DENIED\n    75\t```\n    76\t\n    77\tEvidence-derived conformance has been proven for `NSC-023`, but a conformant task does not establish dependency readiness or execution authority.\n    78\t\n    79\t## Architecture Review Result\n    80\t\n    81\tThe post-Milestone-1 architecture review evaluated one frozen repository commit through eight independent specialist reviews, a synthesis, and an adversarial critique of that synthesis.\n    82\t\n    83\tResult:\n    84\t\n    85\t- eight of eight independent reviewers: `partially_unsound`;\n    86\t- synthesis: `partially_unsound`;\n    87\t- adversarial critique: `synthesis_needs_revision`.\n    88\t\n    89\tAccepted correction direction:\n    90\t\n    91\t- preserve bounded workers, deterministic validation, GER repair, runtime evidence, human authority, and persistent task contracts;\n    92\t- stop treating mutable task metadata as completion truth;\n    93\t- distinguish historical delivery evidence from current conformance;\n    94\t- use reconciliation for bootstrap, broad audits, and reviewed change-impact proposals rather than routine global truth regeneration;\n    95\t- avoid implementing the entire original Milestone 2 bundle before proving smaller real delivery loops;\n    96\t- keep one worker and human merge/design authority until the one-ticket process is trustworthy;\n    97\t- do not treat reviewer consensus or task-graph ordering as automatic product priority.\n    98\t\n    99\tAccepted immutable review evidence is preserved under:\n   100\t\n   101\t`Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/evidence/20260821T222222Z-40fdf9ce/`\n   102\t\n   103\tThat directory contains the frozen manifest, model assignments, all eight independent reviews, synthesis, and adversarial critique.\n   104\t\n   105\tGenerated `Pipeline/ArchitectureReview/outputs/` remains transient and ignored by default.\n   106\t\n   107\t## Architecture Correction Phase 1 — COMPLETE\n   108\t\n   109\tPhase 1 prevented the legacy task model from authorizing autonomous work before the task-contract migration and evidence model existed.\n   110\t\n   111\tKey invariant retained:\n   112\t\n   113\t> Mutable task metadata cannot authorize execution.\n   114\t\n   115\tImplemented safeguards include:\n   116\t\n   117\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/execution_authority.py`;\n   118\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/phase1_execution_authority_smoke_test.py`;\n   119\t- fail-closed authorization behavior in `taskcontrol`.\n   120\t\n   121\tPhase 2 removed operational completion status from the contracts themselves. Phase 3A now provides current-state inspection, but execution authority intentionally remains disabled.\n   122\t\n   123\t## Architecture Correction Phase 2 — COMPLETE\n   124\t\n   125\tAll 37 persistent task records were migrated from schema 1.0 to **task-contract schema 2.0**.\n   126\t\n   127\tCurrent graph state:\n   128\t\n   129\t- 37 active contracts;\n   130\t- 0 superseded contracts;\n   131\t- 0 cancelled contracts;\n   132\t- 36 parent edges;\n   133\t- 59 dependency edges;\n   134\t- 7 exclusive-resource groups;\n   135\t- 17 non-code project requirements;\n   136\t- 75 completion gates;\n   137\t- 2 downstream integration obligations;\n   138\t- one root: `NSC-001` / `no-safe-circle`.\n   139\t\n   140\tSchema 2.0 defines:\n   141\t\n   142\t```text\n   143\tTasks/*.yaml = approved definition of work\n   144\t```\n   145\t\n   146\tnot:\n   147\t\n   148\t```text\n   149\tTasks/*.yaml = definition + running state + validation state + completion truth\n   150\t```\n   151\t\n   152\tEach contract contains:\n   153\t\n   154\t- `schema_version: \"2.0\"`;\n   155\t- `contract_revision`;\n   156\t- `contract_disposition` (`active`, `superseded`, or `cancelled`);\n   157\t- stable acceptance-criterion IDs;\n   158\t- stable completion-gate IDs;\n   159\t- separate downstream integration obligations;\n   160\t- per-contract provenance.\n   161\t\n   162\tTop-level mutable `status` is gone. Historical bootstrap status is retained only as provenance.\n   163\t\n   164\tReviewed migration identity:\n   165\t\n   166\t`task-contract-schema-v2-20260822-r2`\n   167\t\n   168\tImportant reviewed corrections:\n   169\t\n   170\t- NSC-003 duplicate suspend criteria merged;\n   171\t- NSC-003 future pointer-consumer validation moved to a downstream obligation;\n   172\t- NSC-019 duplicate suspend criteria merged;\n   173\t- NSC-019 duplicate reset criteria merged;\n   174\t- NSC-023 fixed-camera checks remain completion gates;\n   175\t- NSC-023 future visual-foundation compatibility remains a downstream obligation.\n   176\t\n   177\tProduction-specific migration overrides are bound to the exact task ID, reconciliation key, and approved bootstrap reconciliation/verification provenance. Synthetic tasks that merely reuse an NSC numeric ID do not receive production-specific corrections.\n   178\t\n   179\tThe migration-report verifier was also made portable across Git-for-Windows line endings: migrated target verification normalizes UTF-8 BOM/CRLF/lone-CR differences while source hashes and pre-publication concurrent-change checks remain byte-exact.\n   180\t\n   181\tThe reviewed migration and quality audit currently pass against all 37 contracts.\n   182\t\n   183\t## Milestone 1 Bootstrap State Retained\n   184\t\n   185\tThe initial human-approved, independently verified bootstrap remains valid historical provenance.\n   186\t\n   187\tSource reconciliation:\n   188\t\n   189\t`20260821T193541Z-998ee7b5`\n   190\t\n   191\tSuccessful verification:\n   192\t\n   193\t`20260821T195959Z-43dba5de`\n   194\t\n   195\tThe bootstrap originally produced:\n   196\t\n   197\t- 37 persistent records;\n   198\t- 12 feature records;\n   199\t- 25 implementation records;\n   200\t- 36 historical `open` observations;\n   201\t- 1 historical `complete` observation;\n   202\t- 59 dependency edges;\n   203\t- 36 parent edges;\n   204\t- 7 exclusive-resource groups;\n   205\t- 17 non-code project requirements.\n   206\t\n   207\tThe only task historically observed as `complete` at bootstrap was:\n   208\t\n   209\t`NSC-023 — Fixed Isometric Camera`\n   210\t\n   211\tThat historical observation was **not** Phase 3 conformance evidence. Before the baseline record was committed, the evaluator correctly ignored the uncommitted evidence and reported NSC-023 as `not_delivered`. Once the evidence was committed, the derived state changed to `conformant`.\n   212\t\n   213\t## Production GDD RAG — CURRENT AND VALIDATED\n   214\t\n   215\t`Pipeline/GDDRAG` is the current production retrieval layer for the canonical GDD.\n   216\t\n   217\tCanonical source:\n   218\t\n   219\t`Docs/GDD/No_Safe_Circle_GDD.md`\n   220\t\n   221\tCurrent production index:\n   222\t\n   223\t`Pipeline/GDDRAG/knowledge_base/No_Safe_Circle_GDD_RAG.json`\n   224\t\n   225\tCurrent production state:\n   226\t\n   227\t- 41 deterministic chunks;\n   228\t- source SHA-256 matches the canonical GDD;\n   229\t- `gddctl status` reports `CURRENT`;\n   230\t- `gddctl validate` passes;\n   231\t- direct `GDDRetriever` consumers enforce the same freshness/integrity boundary.\n   232\t\n   233\tThe production integrity boundary verifies canonical path/line ranges, chunk text, character counts, SHA-256 values, canonical/domain flags, and equivalence to a deterministic rebuild.\n   234\t\n   235\tThe historical Assignment 4 RAG under `DynamicContentPipeline/` remains course output built from the older July GDD and is not trusted as current production canon.\n   236\t\n   237\t### RAG authority boundary\n   238\t\n   239\tThe production RAG solves freshness, provenance, integrity, and deterministic rebuild concerns. It does **not** prove that a top-k result set contains every cross-cutting requirement governing a task.\n   240\t\n   241\tTherefore:\n   242\t\n   243\t```text\n   244\tCanonical GDD = game-design authority\n   245\tProduction GDDRAG = validated search, discovery, and navigation aid\n   246\tTop-k retrieval alone != complete task canon\n   247\t```\n   248\t\n   249\tBecause the GDD is still relatively small and highly cross-linked, bounded implementation workers should receive the whole current GDD until context-pack coverage is independently proven.\n   250\t\n   251\t## Architecture Correction Phase 3A — IMPLEMENTED AND TESTED\n   252\t\n   253\t### Purpose\n   254\t\n   255\tPhase 3A answers:\n   256\t\n   257\t> What does committed repository evidence currently prove about this exact task contract?\n   258\t\n   259\tIt does not answer:\n   260\t\n   261\t> May an autonomous worker execute this task?\n   262\t\n   263\tThose are intentionally separate authority questions.\n   264\t\n   265\t### New Phase 3A files\n   266\t\n   267\t- `Docs/AI-Pipeline/ADR-033_EVIDENCE_DERIVED_CONFORMANCE.md`;\n   268\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/CONFORMANCE_RECORDS.md`;\n   269\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_records.py`;\n   270\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/current_conformance.py`;\n   271\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/conformance_evaluator_smoke_test.py`.\n   272\t\n   273\tUpdated Phase 3A integration files include:\n   274\t\n   275\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py`;\n   276\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/execution_authority.py`;\n   277\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol_smoke_test.py`;\n   278\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/phase1_execution_authority_smoke_test.py`;\n   279\t- `Pipeline/TaskGraph/README.md`.\n   280\t\n   281\t### Evidence record model\n   282\t\n   283\tPhase 3A defines immutable records under:\n   284\t\n   285\t```text\n   286\tPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/<TASK-ID>/records/<RECORD-ID>.json\n   287\t```\n   288\t\n   289\twith committed artifacts conventionally stored under:\n   290\t\n   291\t```text\n   292\tPipeline/TaskGraph/evidence/<TASK-ID>/artifacts/\n   293\t```\n   294\t\n   295\tSupported record types:\n   296\t\n   297\t- `delivery`;\n   298\t- `baseline`;\n   299\t- `revalidation`.\n   300\t\n   301\tRecords bind evidence to:\n   302\t\n   303\t- exact task ID;\n   304\t- task-contract revision and semantic canonical JSON hash;\n   305\t- canonical GDD path and normalized text hash;\n   306\t- validated Git commit and tree;\n   307\t- completion-gate results;\n   308\t- conformance-surface Git blob SHAs;\n   309\t- committed evidence-artifact blob SHAs;\n   310\t- required human approval;\n   311\t- delivery or revalidation lineage.\n   312\t\n   313\tDelivery records require their validated commit/tree to equal the recorded integrated commit/tree.\n   314\t\n   315\tRevalidation records require an existing same-task basis record and valid ancestry with no revalidation cycle.\n   316\t\n   317\tRecords cannot recreate mutable authority fields such as `status`, `complete`, `current`, `ready`, or `authorized`.\n   318\t\n   319\t### Repository authority boundary\n   320\t\n   321\tThe evaluator reads contracts, GDD text, evidence records, and evidence artifacts from **committed Git objects at HEAD**, not from uncommitted working-copy contents.\n   322\t\n   323\tA dirty working tree is reported as a warning, but it does not alter the derived state of committed HEAD.\n   324\t\n   325\tThis was demonstrated directly: while Phase 3A source files were still uncommitted, `taskcontrol state` reported committed HEAD and warned that the working tree was dirty.\n   326\t\n   327\t### Deterministic current-state checks\n   328\t\n   329\tA record can support conformance only when the evaluator can verify the required Git and evidence relationships, including:\n   330\t\n   331\t- validated commit exists and is ancestral to current HEAD;\n   332\t- recorded tree matches the actual tree of the validated commit;\n   333\t- task-contract revision/hash is correct at the validated commit and current HEAD;\n   334\t- canonical GDD hash is correct at the validated commit and current HEAD;\n   335\t- every current completion gate has exactly one passing gate result;\n   336\t- evidence artifacts are committed and match recorded Git blob SHAs;\n   337\t- conformance surfaces match both the validated commit and current HEAD;\n   338\t- required human approval exists;\n   339\t- revalidation basis records are valid and acyclic;\n   340\t- duplicate, malformed, contradictory, or incomparable evidence is not silently accepted.\n   341\t\n   342\t### Derived states\n   343\t\n   344\tPhase 3A implements deterministic states including:\n   345\t\n   346\t- `aggregate`;\n   347\t- `not_delivered`;\n   348\t- `conformant`;\n   349\t- `needs_revalidation`;\n   350\t- `needs_replan`;\n   351\t- `needs_human`;\n   352\t- `invalid_evidence`;\n   353\t- `ambiguous_evidence`;\n   354\t- `superseded`;\n   355\t- `cancelled`.\n   356\t\n   357\tWhen multiple otherwise-current records exist, Git ancestry is used to select a strict descendant. Incomparable maximal records produce `ambiguous_evidence`; timestamps do not decide authority.\n   358\t\n   359\t### First real production conformance proof\n   360\t\n   361\tHuman-readable:\n   362\t\n   363\t```powershell\n   364\tdocker compose run --rm codex-review python3 Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py state NSC-023\n   365\t```\n   366\t\n   367\tStructured output:\n   368\t\n   369\t```powershell\n   370\tdocker compose run --rm codex-review python3 Pipeline/TaskGraph/taskcontrol.py state NSC-023 --json\n   371\t```\n   372\t\n   373\tCurrent real-project result:\n   374\t\n   375\t```text\n   376\tNSC-001 -> aggregate\n   377\tNSC-023 -> conformant\n   378\t```\n   379\t\n   380\tNSC-023 selects:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
